  "content": "# Alabaster — context repository\n\n**A sample, not a real student's work.** Jo Lindqvist is invented, and so is everything here that isn't a public document. The poem, its two versions, Susan Dickinson's objection, and the books in `secondary/` are real. Written for TDM 155AI week 9 so the ensemble has something with several kinds of context to read: primary documents, secondary criticism, the student's own notes, process logs, AI output, and a few things that shouldn't be read at all.\n\n## The piece\n\n*Alabaster*: three letterpress broadsides and a 90-second stop-motion film. The broadsides set the two versions of Emily Dickinson's \"Safe in their Alabaster Chambers\" (1859 and 1861) and, on the third sheet, Susan Dickinson's one-line objection to the second verse. The film is the type for the second stanza being pulled apart and reset, sort by sort, from the 1859 version to the 1861 one. Finished October 30; the film is in `piece/` on the shared drive (not here).\n\n## Where to start\n\n1. `piece/description.md` — what it is and why.\n2. `notebook/dictated-2026-10-06.md` — where the idea came from.\n3. `process/edit-log.md` — how the film was cut.\n4. `primary/` — the poem, both versions, and the letter.\n\n## What's here\n\n- `primary/` — my transcriptions of the two versions, the publication in the *Springfield Republican*, and Sue's note.\n- `secondary/` — reading notes on Cameron, Howe, Miller, and Werner and Bervin, and one AI summary I asked for and did not check.\n- `notebook/` — week 5 research and dictation, and my running questions.\n- `process/` — the typesetting studies from week 6, the edit log from week 7, and the prompts and scripts I used.\n- `piece/` — the description and the colophon.\n- `slack/` — my weekly posts.\n- `credits.md`.\n- `private/` — not for the podcast. See the note inside.\n"
