  "content": "---\ntitle: Alabaster — what it is\nauthor: Jo Lindqvist (invented; sample)\n---\n\nThree broadsides, 11 by 17, Caslon, printed on the Vandercook in the Bow and Arrow Press basement, edition of 12. Sheet one: the 1859 version of \"Safe in their Alabaster Chambers.\" Sheet two: the 1861 version, with the second stanza Emily sent to Sue after Sue objected. Sheet three: Sue's sentence, alone, small, in the middle of the page: \"I am not suited dear Emily with the second verse.\"\n\nThe film: 90 seconds, top-down, one frame per sort. The second stanza of 1859 is locked in the chase. Hands take it apart letter by letter and set the 1861 stanza in its place. No voiceover. Room tone from the press room and the sound of type on the stone.\n\nWhat I wanted: to make the variant physical. In the manuscripts a variant is a little cross and a word in the margin. On a press, changing a word means pulling actual metal out and putting other metal in, and the two stanzas don't take the same amount of space, so the whole form shifts. Sharon Cameron says Dickinson \"chose not to choose\" between variants. I wanted to see what it costs to choose.\n\nWhere it stands: printed and filmed. I'd reprint sheet three; the impression is too heavy.\n"
