  "path": "secondary/ai-summary-of-cameron.md",
  "content": "---\ntitle: AI summary of Cameron's Choosing Not Choosing\nauthor: Claude (model output), asked for by Jo on 2026-10-08\nstatus: NOT CHECKED against the book. I asked for this before I'd read it; some of it may be wrong.\n---\n\nSharon Cameron's *Choosing Not Choosing* (University of Chicago Press, 1992) argues that Emily Dickinson's fascicles, the hand-sewn booklets into which she copied her poems, should be read as deliberate compositions rather than as storage. Cameron focuses on the variant words Dickinson recorded in the margins and at the ends of poems, marked with small crosses. Editors from Mabel Loomis Todd and Thomas Wentworth Higginson through Thomas H. Johnson treated these as unresolved drafts and chose among them; Cameron argues that the refusal to choose is itself the poem's mode. She extends this to the fascicle as a unit, suggesting that poems placed together in a fascicle read one another, so that the boundary of \"the poem\" is also left open. The book's central example chapters address Fascicles 15 and 16 among others, and the argument has been influential on later manuscript-centered scholarship, including the work of Marta Werner and the digital editions.\n\n[Jo's note: the bit about which fascicles the chapters cover is the part I'd check first. Also whether Todd and Higginson \"chose\" or just cut.]\n"
